`.iter().min()` needs `Ord`, but the bound is only `PartialOrd` (so this must also work for floats). Fold with a manual `<` compare instead.
```rust
fn min_item<T: PartialOrd + Copy>(items: &[T]) -> Option<T> {
    let mut it = items.iter().copied();
    let first = it.next()?;
    Some(it.fold(first, |acc, x| if x < acc { x } else { acc }))
}
```

`f64` is not `Ord` (NaN), so `.sort()` won't compile — use `sort_by` with `partial_cmp`. For descending, compare `b` to `a`.
```rust
fn sort_desc_floats(v: &[f64]) -> Vec<f64> {
    let mut out = v.to_vec();
    out.sort_by(|a, b| b.partial_cmp(a).unwrap());
    out
}
```

To reassign the parameter `n` inside the body it must be `mut`; a bare `n` parameter is immutable.
```rust
fn decrement_to_zero(mut n: u32) -> Vec<u32> {
    let mut out = Vec::new();
    loop {
        out.push(n);
        if n == 0 { break; }
        n -= 1;
    }
    out
}
```

Iterate `chars()` and compare by value; passing `&char` to a `str` method expecting a `Pattern` won't compile, so don't reach for `matches`.
```rust
fn count_char(s: &str, target: char) -> usize {
    s.chars().filter(|&c| c == target).count()
}
```

Dedup via a sorted copy, then index. Guard the length before indexing so it never panics.
```rust
fn second_smallest(v: &[i32]) -> Option<i32> {
    let mut s = v.to_vec();
    s.sort();
    s.dedup();
    if s.len() < 2 { None } else { Some(s[1]) }
}
```

Use the entry API; `*entry.or_insert(0) += 1` keeps the value type `usize` so there is no type mismatch.
```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
fn char_frequencies(s: &str) -> HashMap<char, usize> {
    let mut m: HashMap<char, usize> = HashMap::new();
    for c in s.chars() { *m.entry(c).or_insert(0) += 1; }
    m
}
```

Count into a map, then pick the max by COUNT with `max_by_key`. Counts are `usize` which is `Ord`, so `max_by_key` is fine here.
```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
fn mode_of(v: &[i32]) -> Option<i32> {
    if v.is_empty() { return None; }
    let mut counts: HashMap<i32, usize> = HashMap::new();
    for &x in v { *counts.entry(x).or_insert(0) += 1; }
    counts.into_iter().max_by_key(|&(_, c)| c).map(|(val, _)| val)
}
```

Pop the first char from the iterator, uppercase it, then append the remainder of the string.
```rust
fn capitalize_first(s: &str) -> String {
    let mut chars = s.chars();
    match chars.next() {
        Some(first) => first.to_uppercase().collect::<String>() + chars.as_str(),
        None => String::new(),
    }
}
```

`max_by_key` on word length returns the LAST max on ties; to get the first, fold manually keeping strictly-greater.
```rust
fn longest_word(s: &str) -> Option<String> {
    s.split_whitespace().fold(None, |best: Option<&str>, w| match best {
        Some(b) if w.len() > b.len() => Some(w),
        Some(b) => Some(b),
        None => Some(w),
    }).map(|w| w.to_string())
}
```

Compare adjacent pairs with `windows(2)`; an empty or single-element slice is trivially sorted (the iterator is empty, so `all` returns true).
```rust
fn is_sorted_asc(v: &[i32]) -> bool {
    v.windows(2).all(|w| w[0] <= w[1])
}
```

Sort a deduped copy descending, then index at n-1 after a bounds check so it can't panic.
```rust
fn nth_largest(v: &[i32], n: usize) -> Option<i32> {
    if n == 0 { return None; }
    let mut s = v.to_vec();
    s.sort_unstable_by(|a, b| b.cmp(a));
    s.dedup();
    s.get(n - 1).copied()
}
```

`scan` threads a mutable accumulator and yields each running total.
```rust
fn running_sum(v: &[i32]) -> Vec<i32> {
    v.iter().scan(0, |acc, &x| { *acc += x; Some(*acc) }).collect()
}
```

The standard formatter handles this directly; reimplementing risks an empty-string edge case for zero.
```rust
fn to_hex(n: u32) -> String {
    format!("{:x}", n)
}
```

Walk once, starting a new group whenever the value changes. The return type is `Vec<Vec<i32>>`, so push owned inner vecs.
```rust
fn group_consecutive(v: &[i32]) -> Vec<Vec<i32>> {
    let mut out: Vec<Vec<i32>> = Vec::new();
    for &x in v {
        match out.last_mut() {
            Some(g) if *g.last().unwrap() == x => g.push(x),
            _ => out.push(vec![x]),
        }
    }
    out
}
```

Cast the integer sum and length to `f64` before dividing so the result keeps its fractional part.
```rust
fn average(v: &[i32]) -> Option<f64> {
    if v.is_empty() { return None; }
    let sum: i64 = v.iter().map(|&x| x as i64).sum();
    Some(sum as f64 / v.len() as f64)
}
```

Map each char: ASCII-uppercase letters become lowercase and vice versa; leave others as-is.
```rust
fn swap_case(s: &str) -> String {
    s.chars().map(|c| {
        if c.is_ascii_uppercase() { c.to_ascii_lowercase() }
        else if c.is_ascii_lowercase() { c.to_ascii_uppercase() }
        else { c }
    }).collect()
}
```

Push a value only when it differs from the last pushed one.
```rust
fn unique_in_order(v: &[i32]) -> Vec<i32> {
    let mut out: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();
    for &x in v {
        if out.last() != Some(&x) { out.push(x); }
    }
    out
}
```

Repeatedly take `n % 10` and divide; the parameter is `mut` so it can be reduced in place.
```rust
fn digit_sum(mut n: u64) -> u64 {
    let mut s = 0;
    while n > 0 { s += n % 10; n /= 10; }
    s
}
```

`windows(2)` yields adjacent slices; map each to its sum. The return type is `Vec<i32>`, so collect the summed values, not the windows.
```rust
fn pair_sums(v: &[i32]) -> Vec<i32> {
    v.windows(2).map(|w| w[0] + w[1]).collect()
}
```

`i32` implements `Ord`, so `Ord::clamp` is available directly.
```rust
fn clamp_to(v: &[i32], lo: i32, hi: i32) -> Vec<i32> {
    v.iter().map(|&x| x.clamp(lo, hi)).collect()
}
```

`T::max` requires `Ord`; with only `PartialOrd` use an explicit comparison.
```rust
fn max_pair<T: PartialOrd + Copy>(a: T, b: T) -> T {
    if a >= b { a } else { b }
}
```

Sort a copy descending, then take k. `take` already stops at the end, so no bounds math is needed.
```rust
fn top_k(v: &[i32], k: usize) -> Vec<i32> {
    let mut s = v.to_vec();
    s.sort_unstable_by(|a, b| b.cmp(a));
    s.into_iter().take(k).collect()
}
```

Count first, then scan in order for the first count of 1 — two passes keeps it simple and correct.
```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
fn first_non_repeating(s: &str) -> Option<char> {
    let mut counts: HashMap<char, usize> = HashMap::new();
    for c in s.chars() { *counts.entry(c).or_insert(0) += 1; }
    s.chars().find(|c| counts[c] == 1)
}
```

`f64` isn't `Ord`, so reduce with explicit comparisons rather than `.iter().max()`. Track min and max in one pass.
```rust
fn range_of(v: &[f64]) -> Option<f64> {
    let mut it = v.iter().copied();
    let first = it.next()?;
    let (mut lo, mut hi) = (first, first);
    for x in it {
        if x < lo { lo = x; }
        if x > hi { hi = x; }
    }
    Some(hi - lo)
}
```
